course: money
lesson: 42
title: Olympiad: saving plans and offers too good to be true ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This last olympiad lesson mixes saving up, piggy-bank jars, bank interest, subscriptions, loans and scams into puzzles where you must do the sum and also smell a trick.
explain: A saving plan is a simple sum: take what you still need and divide by what you save each week - for a £30 kit with £4 a week that is 7 and a half, so you need 8 weeks, because there is no half a pocket-money day.
explain: Picture a stranger's advert: "Brand-new trainers worth £400, today only £10!" No real shop sells £400 trainers for £10, so they are fake, stolen or a trick to get your card details - if it sounds too good to be true, it almost always is.
explain: Small amounts add up both ways: 50p saved every week makes £26 in a year, and a £9-a-month app left running makes £108 in a year.
explain: Listen past the talk: "I've saved a million quid" is not the number to use, "a tad pricey" may hide a loan that costs half as much again, and when someone says "£5 - no wait, £4" the last number counts.
fact: Weeks to reach a goal = money still needed divided by the money saved each week, rounded UP to a whole week.
fact: A year has 52 weeks and 12 months.
fact: A plan that grows by £1 every week (£1, £2, £3...) starts slower than a steady £3 a week, catches up after 5 weeks (£15 each), and is ahead after that.
fact: Doubling grows very fast: 1p, 2p, 4p, 8p... puts in £5.12 on the 10th day, and the 10 days together make £10.23.
fact: A savings account pays a little interest, told as "£2 for every £100 a year": £250 earns £5 in a year.
fact: A free trial turns into a paid subscription unless you cancel it in time.
fact: Paying a little each week for a long time (a loan or "pay later") usually costs more in total than paying the price at once.
fact: A real prize never asks you to pay a fee or give card details before you get it.
fact: "Double your money, guaranteed" and "free skins, just log in with your password" are well-known scams.
fact: Splitting pocket money into jars works with fractions: from £10, a half to spend is £5 and a fifth for charity is £2, which leaves £3 to save.
fact: A "was" price only shows a real saving if the shop really charged it for a good while before.
fact: Slang in this lesson: "mid" means not very good, "sweet as" (New Zealand) means great, "yer man" (Ireland) means that man, "grand" (Ireland) means fine, "no cap" means honestly, "a tad" means a little, and "back in my day" is how older people start a story about the past.
exercise: Ella has £6 and saves £5 a week for a £36 game. How many weeks must she save? | 6 | 7 | 8 | 5
exercise: Tom saves £4 a week for £30 headphones and starts with nothing. How many whole weeks does he need? | 8 | 7 | 7 and a half | 9
exercise: Jo saves a fifth of her £10 pocket money every week. How much has she saved after 6 weeks? | £12 | £10 | £20 | £8
exercise: Plan A saves £3 every week; Plan B saves £1 in week 1, £2 in week 2, £3 in week 3 and so on. After 5 weeks, which has more? | They have the same, £15 each | Plan A, £15 against £10 | Plan B, £21 against £15 | Plan A, £18 against £15
exercise: Put 1p in a jar on day 1, 2p on day 2, 4p on day 3, doubling every day. How much goes in on day 8? | £1.28 | 16p | 64p | £2.56
exercise: A bank pays £2 for every £100 a year. How much does £250 earn in a year? | £5 | £2 | £4 | £50
exercise: An app is free for the first month, then £8 a month. If you forget to cancel, what have you paid after 12 months? | £88 | £96 | £8 | £80
exercise: A text says, "You've won £500! Just pay a £10 fee to claim it." What is the biggest clue? | Real prizes never ask you to pay to get them | £10 is small, so it must be real | A £10 fee is fair for a £500 prize | You must pay fast before others do
exercise: A bike is £150, or £5 a week for 40 weeks. How much extra does paying weekly cost? | £50 | £150 | £200 | £5
exercise: Trainers say "Half price! Was £60, now £30", but last month they were £30 too. What is the real saving? | £0 | £30 | £15 | £60
exercise: An advert says, "Double your money in a week, guaranteed!" What is most likely to happen to £20 sent to it? | It is lost, because the advert is a scam | It becomes £40 next week, as promised | It comes back with a fifth on top | It is kept safe like money in a bank
exercise: From £10 pocket money, Sam spends a half and gives a fifth to charity. How much is left to save? | £3 | £5 | £2 | £8
exercise: A game pass is £5 a month, or £50 for a whole year paid at once. How much does paying for the year save? | £10 | £5 | £50 | £60
exercise: Mia saves £2 every week, but every 4th week she takes £3 out for a treat. What is in her jar after 8 weeks? | £10 | £16 | £13 | £6
exercise: A phone is "free" on a plan of £20 a month for 24 months. What do you really pay? | £480 | £0 | £240 | £44
exercise: Which offer is most likely too good to be true? | New £400 trainers for £10 from a pop-up advert | Last year's trainers with a fifth off in a big shop | Trainers on a clearance rail with a quarter off | A second pair at half price in a shoe shop
exercise: Ben wants £50 and saves £3 a week; Gran gives him £14 on his birthday first. How many weeks must he save? | 12 | 17 | 14 | 11
test: ★★★ Ruby: "I've saved like a million quid - well, £18 - and I need £30 for the kit. I put in £4 a week." How many more weeks? | 3 | 12 | 30 | 7 and a half
test: ★★★ A text: "CONGRATS u won an iPhone!! just pay £2 delivery, enter card details." Big bro: "sound, only two quid." What's the truth? | It's a scam to get the card details | It's real, delivery costs £2 | It's fine, the phone is free | It's real, it says congrats
test: ★★★ Leo: "Plan A's £3 a week, Plan B starts at £1 and adds £1 every week. After 6 weeks Plan A wins, innit." Is he right? | No: Plan B has £21, Plan A £18 | Yes: Plan A has £18, Plan B only £15 | No: they both have exactly £18 each | Yes: Plan A has £21, Plan B £18
test: ★★★ Uncle Pete: "TV's £200, or £10 a week for 30 weeks. Bit pricey, that loan." How much extra does the loan really cost? | £100 | £10 | £30 | £300
test: ★★★ Jayden, 9: "this app's giving free skins, no cap, just log in with ur password." What is the real risk? | Someone steals the password and the account | He gets too many skins at once | The skins vanish after a week | Nothing, logging in is safe
test: ★★★ Grace: "I save £5 - no wait, £4 - every week. How much in 10 weeks?" | £40 | £50 | £45 | £14
test: ★★★ Aoife in Galway: "Yer man at the market says a tenner for a real gold watch that's £1,000 in the shops. Grand, so?" What is most likely? | It's fake or stolen, so walk away | It's a real bargain, so buy two of them | Market prices are always a tenth of shop prices | Gold watches have got very cheap this year
test: ★★★ Noah: "Bank gives £3 for every £100 a year. I've got £400 in, so I'll get £3, mid." What will he really get? | £12 | £3 | £7 | £43
test: ★★★ Little sister: "Give me 1p today, 2p tomorrow, double every day for 10 days - or just £5 now." Which is more? | The doubling, £10.23 in all | The £5 now, way more | The doubling, just 20p in all | They are exactly the same
test: ★★★ Mum's music app: "only £1 for the first month, then £9 a month, sweet as." What does a whole year cost? | £100 | £12 | £108 | £99
test: ★★★ Grandad: "Back in my day sweets were a penny." He puts 50p a week in a jar for a year. How much is that? | £26 | £25 | £52 | £50
exam: It is given that a pupil possessing six pounds saves five pounds weekly toward a game of thirty-six pounds. Ascertain the number of weeks required. | Six | Seven | Eight | Five
exam: It is given that four pounds is saved weekly toward a sum of thirty pounds. Determine the least number of whole weeks required. | Eight | Seven | Seven and a half | Nine
exam: A subscription is free for the first month and costs eight pounds monthly thereafter. Ascertain the total paid over twelve months. | Eighty-eight pounds | Ninety-six pounds | Eight pounds | Eighty pounds
exam: Identify the surest sign that a prize is fraudulent. | A payment is demanded before it may be claimed | The prize is of modest value | The message arrives by post | The prize was won in a school raffle
